diff --git a/Documentation/technical/diff-line-metadata.adoc b/Documentation/technical/diff-line-metadata.adoc
new file mode 100644
index 0000000000..cb1a57a612
--- /dev/null
+++ b/Documentation/technical/diff-line-metadata.adoc
@@ -0,0 +1,168 @@
+= Diff line metadata
+
+A program that shows a diff in a view of its own -- a text editor, a
+terminal UI, a code review tool -- often wants to act on the line the user
+is pointing at: stage it, open an editor on it, link to it in a review, or
+keep the view anchored on it while the diff is rendered again with
+different options.  Every one of those needs the same thing: given a
+position in the rendered output, which line of the diff is it?
+
+For the unified formats that question can be answered by reading the
+output back: walk up to the enclosing `@@` line and count the `+` and
+context lines down to the position.  A word diff (`--word-diff`,
+`--color-words`) cannot be read back that way.  Its markup is inline, so
+there is no marker column to classify a line by, changes to several source
+lines can share one output line, and a single output line can mix text
+from the old file with text from the new.  We are the only ones who still
+know what each rendered region belongs to.
+
+So we say it, in an OSC escape sequence emitted in front of each region.
+The sequences are invisible in a terminal, which skips an OSC it does not
+know, and are only emitted when a consumer asks for them.
+
+== The handshake
+
+A consumer sets `OSC1717` in our environment to the list of
+protocol versions it understands, as `V<n>` separated by commas:
+
+	OSC1717=V1
+
+We emit the highest version listed that we also know, and nothing at all
+if the variable is unset or names no version we know.  An entry that
+cannot be parsed is ignored rather than taken as a reason to stay silent,
+so that a consumer which lists a version we have never heard of still gets
+an older one.
+
+The handshake exists so that the output is unchanged for everybody else:
+run outside such a consumer, in a terminal, a pipeline, a script or a
+file, we emit nothing.  It is also what makes the format able to change:
+a later version can redefine the payload wholesale, because both sides
+agree on which one they are speaking.
+
+Having negotiated a version, we name it once, before any diff, in a
+record that carries the version and nothing else:
+
+	ESC ] 1717 ; <version> ST
+
+A consumer can therefore learn whether we speak the protocol at all by
+running us on an empty word diff and looking for this record: a diff with
+no content of its own emits no other record, so its absence alone would
+say nothing.  It has to be a word diff, being the only kind we describe:
+asked for any other we say nothing at all, which tells a consumer that
+this diff carries no records but not whether another one would.
+
+== The records
+
+Each record has this form, with `ESC` being 0x1B and `ST` being `ESC \`:
+
+	ESC ] 1717 ; <version> ; <type> ; <new-line> ; <old-line> ; <file> ST
+
+`version`;;
+	The negotiated version, repeated in every record so that a record
+	is self-describing.
+
+`type`;;
+	One character: `c` for a context line, `a` for an added one, `d`
+	for a removed one, `f` for a file header, `h` for a hunk header.  A
+	consumer that meets a type it does not know is expected to ignore
+	the record rather than reject it, so that this set can grow without
+	a new version.
+
+`new-line`;;
+	The line number in the new file, in the numbering of the diff
+	being shown rather than of the file in the working tree -- the
+	diff may be against the index or an older commit.  A removed line
+	carries the position it sits at, which is the same for every line
+	of a run of removals.  On a hunk header it is the first line of
+	the hunk it heads, which is what lets an action on the header land
+	at the top of what the user is looking at.  For a file that the
+	diff deletes it is `0`.  On a file header it is empty: the actions
+	a file header stands for -- open the file, jump to it, list it --
+	need no line, and a renderer that streams its output does not yet
+	know the file's first line when it draws the header.
+
+`old-line`;;
+	The line number in the old file.  Present only for `d`, empty
+	otherwise, so that a removed line is the one kind of record that
+	carries both numbers: one for finding it in the patch, one for
+	opening an editor on it.
+
+`file`;;
+	The path the line belongs to, as the diff names it.  Last on
+	purpose, so that it may itself contain `;`: everything past the
+	fourth `;` is the path.
+
+The type is what the diff makes of the line, not what the rendering makes
+of it: a word diff hands us the words two versions of a line have in
+common, but the line they belong to is still a line the patch adds, so its
+records say `a`.  Were they to say `c`, a consumer staging by them would
+be told the line is unchanged and would stage one half of a modification.
+
+A hunk header carries a record so that the rows between one hunk's content
+and the next are not a gap the consumer has to guess its way across: it
+can tell a hunk boundary from the record rather than from a discontinuity
+in the line numbers, and an action on the header row has somewhere to go.
+
+Every row of a file header carries one too -- `diff --git`, the mode and
+`index` lines, `---` and `+++`, the rename and similarity lines, and the
+`Binary files ... differ` line that stands in for a binary file's content.
+Naming every row rather than only the first means the user can act
+anywhere in a header and land on the same file.  It also means a file with
+no content rows at all -- a mode change, a pure rename, a binary file --
+is named at least once, so that it stays visible to a consumer that builds
+its idea of the diff from the records: a file list includes it, and
+navigation can stop on it, even though there is nothing in it to stage.
+
+A row that belongs to no line and no header at all carries no record, and
+a consumer treats an un-annotated row as one it cannot act on.
+
+== Where they go
+
+A record is emitted immediately before the first cell of the region it
+describes, and describes everything up to the next record or the end of
+the line.  A consumer thus finds the region a position is in by looking
+leftward for the nearest record, and never has to know how the output is
+laid out -- which is the point, as we are the only one who does.
+
+That has three consequences worth naming:
+
+* A line carries as many records as it has regions.  A word diff line
+  showing a word removed in the middle of an added line carries three:
+  the added text before it, the removal, and the added text after it.
+
+* The record goes after anything that is not part of the region, such as
+  the graph prefix drawn by `git log --graph`, and before anything that
+  is, including the coloring of the region.
+
+* Every output line of a region gets one, including the lines after the
+  first when a region spans several, and including a line that a region
+  contributes nothing to but its line break.  A line with no record on it
+  is a line a consumer cannot act on, so the last case matters: it is how
+  an added or removed blank line is described.
+
+== When nothing is emitted
+
+Besides an absent or unusable handshake:
+
+* For any format other than `--word-diff=plain` and `--color-words`.  The
+  unified formats can be read back, and `--word-diff=porcelain` is meant
+  to be parsed already and marks up every line of its output for that
+  purpose; neither needs the records, and the porcelain format would not
+  welcome anything else between its lines.
+
+* When our output goes to a pager.  A pager may be a diff renderer that
+  lays the diff out anew, in which case only it knows where each line
+  ends up and only its records describe what the consumer sees; ours
+  would describe bytes that nothing shows.  Whoever renders last is the
+  one whose records count, so we leave them to it.  A consumer that runs
+  us directly and pipes us into a renderer itself has the same choice to
+  make, and makes it by setting the variable on one of the two.
+
+== Other implementations
+
+The protocol is not ours alone: delta, difftastic and diff-so-fancy
+restructure a diff far more than a word diff does, and emit the same
+records under the same handshake, so that one consumer can read any of
+them.  What is written here is the part of the protocol we implement; a
+renderer that draws its own headers or its own columns has more to say
+than we do.
